pr-list [--interactive|-i]

List the GitHub PRs for the branches currently checked out across a
set of repos, so a multi-repo change lands as one reviewable table.

Mode is auto-detected from the cwd:

  single-repo  cwd is inside a git repo -> just this worktree's
               branch.
  multi-repo   cwd is not a repo but holds git repos as child dirs
               -> one row per repo.

The multi-repo case is the interesting one. `pr-worktree`'s
multi-repo mode creates a COLLECTION dir holding one worktree of
every repo, all on the same branch:

  ~/git/work/StorytelDataPlatform_20260724_DATA-2769-foo/
    data-platform-artifacts/  data-platform-dbt/  ...

Running `pr-list` there gives the status of the whole change set.

For each repo it looks up the PR whose head branch is that
worktree's CURRENT branch, so the table tracks the change set you
are working on rather than every open PR in the repo. Repos with no
PR for their branch show as "no PR" -- that absence is the point:
it is the repo you still have to open a PR for.

Columns:

  REPO     the worktree directory name.
  PR       #number, an OSC 8 hyperlink to the PR.
  STATE    open / draft / merged / closed / no PR (colored).
  CHECKS   check rollup: "ok N", "fail N/M", "pend N/M", or "-".
           SKIPPED and NEUTRAL contexts are excluded from the
           denominator.
  REVIEW   approved / changes-req / review-req, or "-".
  UPDATED  humanized age of the PR's updatedAt ("3d ago").
  TITLE    the PR title.

After the table it prints a "links" block of bare `repo: url`
lines, one per PR, for copy/pasting the whole set into Slack (which
unfurls bare URLs into clickable titled links). These are
deliberately NOT OSC 8 hyperlinks: an OSC 8 link lives in escape
bytes that a terminal renders but a copy-paste does not carry.

Repos are queried in parallel (one background `gh` per repo), so a
7-repo collection costs about as much as a single `gh` call.

Options:

  -i, --interactive  Open an fzf picker instead of printing the
                     table. ENTER opens the selected PR in the
                     browser; the preview pane shows `gh pr view`.
                     Ctrl-P cycles the preview size, Ctrl-G aborts.
                     Rows without a PR are listed but not openable.
  -h, --help         Show a usage line.

Examples:

  pr-list
  pr-list -i

Requires: gh, git, jq (plus fzf for -i).
